No, it doesn't. That condition is a classic bug.

`u.role == 'admin' || 'guest'` evaluates as `(u.role == 'admin') || 'guest'`. The second operand is the string `'guest'`, which is always truthy. So the whole expression is always true, meaning `checkUser` always returns `true` for any input — no matter what role the user has.

You probably meant:

```js
function checkUser(u) {
  if (u.role === 'admin' || u.role === 'guest') return true;
  return false;
}
```

Or more simply:

```js
function checkUser(u) {
  return u.role === 'admin' || u.role === 'guest';
}
```

I switched `==` to `===` on purpose — loose equality does type coercion and bites people. Also note the function returns `undefined` instead of `false` when the condition fails; the version above fixes that too.

