```csharp
public static string InsertChar(string input, char c, int index)
{
    if (input == null)
        throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(input));
    if (index < 0 || index > input.Length)
        throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(index));

    return input.Insert(index, c.ToString());
}
```

`string.Insert` already does the work, so the method is mostly validation. The bounds check matters: `Insert` throws if `index` is outside `0..Length`, and catching that early gives a clearer error.

If you want it without relying on `Insert`, this does the same thing:

```csharp
return input.Substring(0, index) + c + input.Substring(index);
```

