To calculate the median from an array of integers in Java, you can follow these steps:

1. Sort the array to easily find the middle value.
2. If the number of elements is odd, the median is the middle element.
3. If the number of elements is even, the median is the average of the two middle elements.

Here is the code to achieve this:

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class MedianCalculator {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {7, 22, 21, 19, 17, 24};
        Arrays.sort(arr);

        int n = arr.length;
        if (n % 2 == 1) {
            System.out.println("Median: " + arr[n / 2]);
        } else {
            System.out.println("Median: " + (arr[n / 2 - 1] + arr[n / 2]) / 2);
        }
    }
}
```

The median of the array [7, 22, 21, 19, 17, 24] is 21.

```javascript
function randomAluminumHex() {
  // Aluminum greys: equal RGB channels, mid-to-light range
  const base = Math.floor(Math.random() * 96) + 120; // 120–215
  const hex = base.toString(16).padStart(2, '0');
  return '#' + hex + hex + hex;
}

console.log(randomAluminumHex());
```

The trick is that aluminum is a neutral metal, so its color has equal red, green, and blue values. I picked the 120–215 range because that's the silvery-grey zone — darker values look like charcoal, lighter ones wash out to white. If you want more variation, you could add a small random offset (±10) to each channel independently, but that starts drifting toward warm or cool greys rather than true aluminum.

