Dependency injection is when you stop lettin' your classes go out and score their own dependencies, and instead you hand 'em what they need from the outside. No more `new`-ing up your own crew inside the joint.

Say you got a `BankAccount` that needs a `Logger`. The amateur move:

```csharp
public class BankAccount
{
    private readonly Logger _logger = new Logger(); // snitchin' on yourself
}
```

Now `BankAccount` is married to `Logger` forever. Can't swap it, can't test it without draggin' the real thing along.

The made-man move — pass it in:

```csharp
public class BankAccount
{
    private readonly ILogger _logger;
    public BankAccount(ILogger logger) => _logger = logger;
}
```

Now whoever runs the operation decides what `ILogger` gets handed over. In .NET you wire it up in the container:

```csharp
builder.Services.AddScoped<ILogger, FileLogger>();
builder.Services.AddScoped<BankAccount>();
```

The container's the fixer. It sees `BankAccount` needs an `ILogger`, so it supplies one. You just ask for the `BankAccount` and everything's already connected.

Why it matters: you can slip in a fake logger for tests, swap `FileLogger` for `CloudLogger` without touchin' `BankAccount`, and each class only worries about its own business. Loose coupling, clean hands.

