```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

// Board: 8x8, 1 = piece, 0 = empty. Player moves toward row 0.
// A piece has a clear path if no opponent piece blocks its forward diagonals
// on the way to the king row.
bool clearPathToKing(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& board,
                     int row, int col, int player) {
    int dir = (player == 0) ? -1 : 1; // forward direction
    int r = row + dir, c = col;
    while (r >= 0 && r < 8) {
        bool blocked = false;
        // check both forward diagonals at this row
        for (int dc : {-1, 1}) {
            int cc = c + dc;
            if (cc >= 0 && cc < 8 && board[r][cc] != 0) {
                blocked = true;
                break;
            }
        }
        if (blocked) return false;
        r += dir;
    }
    return true;
}
```

This checks whether any opponent piece sits on either forward diagonal between the piece and the king row. It runs in O(8) time per piece, O(1) space. For a full board scan, call it for each of your pieces.

Note: this treats any occupied square as blocking. If you want to allow jumping over a single opponent piece (standard checkers capture), the logic needs to track landing squares instead.

